<body>

<header class="hero">
  <h1>Nine Decades of Oscar-Winning Scores</h1>
  <p class="subtitle">A Historical Trend Analysis of the Academy Award for Best Original Score<br>1934 – 2026 &nbsp;·&nbsp; 93 Ceremonies &nbsp;·&nbsp; 79 Composers</p>
</header>

<div class="container">

<!-- KPI Summary -->
<div class="kpi-grid">
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="kpi-value">79</div>
    <div class="kpi-label">Total Unique<br>Composers</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="kpi-value">4</div>
    <div class="kpi-label">Maximum Wins by a<br>Single Composer</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="kpi-value">28.0%</div>
    <div class="kpi-label">Repeat Winner Rate<br>26 of 93 Ceremonies</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="kpi-value">1940s &amp; 1980s</div>
    <div class="kpi-label">Decades with Most<br>Unique Winners (12 each)</div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- Section 1: Dataset Overview -->
<section class="section" id="overview">
  <h2>Dataset Overview &amp; Historical Context</h2>
  <p class="section-meta">Scope, structure, and the story behind 93 years of Oscar-winning scores</p>

  <p class="lead">This analysis draws on a complete record of every Best Original Score winner from the 6th Academy Awards ceremony in <em>1934</em> through the 98th ceremony in <em>March 2026</em>. The dataset comprises <em>93 rows</em> across three dimensions — <em>Year</em>, <em>Film</em>, and <em>Composer</em> — with no missing values and composer names standardized for consistency.</p>

  <p>The category has evolved considerably over nine decades. In select years the Academy split the award into subcategories (dramatic vs. comedy/musical scores); this dataset consolidates to one winner per ceremony year, providing a clean longitudinal record. Across the 93 ceremonies, <em>92 unique films</em> earned the award — <em>Oppenheimer</em> (Ludwig Göransson) appears twice, winning in both 2023 and 2024, though the Academy's category consolidation means these represent distinct ceremony years.</p>

  <p>Of the <em>79 unique composers</em> who have won, <em>17 have won more than once</em>, accounting for 31 of the 93 awards. The remaining 62 awards went to one-time winners — a reminder that for most composers, a single Oscar represents a career-defining achievement. The repeat-winner phenomenon is concentrated: the top three composers alone (each with 4 wins) claim 12 of the 93 statuettes, or <em>12.9%</em> of all Best Original Score awards ever given.</p>

  <div class="highlight-box">
    <p><strong>93 ceremonies</strong> spanning <strong>1934 to 2026</strong></p>
    <p><strong>79 distinct composers</strong> honored, across <strong>92 unique films</strong></p>
    <p><strong>8 shared awards</strong> where multiple composers won together in the same ceremony</p>
    <p><strong>17 repeat winners</strong> (21.5% of all winning composers), claiming 31 of 93 awards (33.3%)</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- Section 2: Most Awarded Composers -->
<section class="section" id="top-composers">
  <h2>Most Awarded Composers</h2>
  <p class="section-meta">The dominant figures in Oscar scoring history and what their trajectories reveal</p>

  <p class="lead">Three composers share the all-time record — <em>John Barry</em>, <em>John Williams</em>, and <em>Ludwig Göransson</em> — each with <em>4 Best Original Score Oscars</em>. But their paths to four wins could not be more different: Barry spread his across 24 years (1966–1990), Williams across 18 years (1975–1993), and Göransson compressed his into just 8 years (2018–2026).</p>

  <div class="two-col">
    <div>
      <table class="ranking-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Rank</th><th>Composer</th><th>Wins</th><th>Span</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr class="winner"><td class="rank-num">1</td><td class="composer-name">John Barry</td><td>4</td><td>1966–1990</td></tr>
          <tr class="winner"><td class="rank-num">1</td><td class="composer-name">John Williams</td><td>4</td><td>1975–1993</td></tr>
          <tr class="winner"><td class="rank-num">1</td><td class="composer-name">Ludwig Göransson</td><td>4</td><td>2018–2026</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="rank-num">4</td><td class="composer-name">Max Steiner</td><td>3</td><td>1935–1945</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="rank-num">4</td><td class="composer-name">Dimitri Tiomkin</td><td>3</td><td>1952–1958</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="rank-num">4</td><td class="composer-name">Maurice Jarre</td><td>3</td><td>1962–1984</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="rank-num">4</td><td class="composer-name">Alan Menken</td><td>3</td><td>1989–1992</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="rank-num">8</td><td class="composer-name">Alfred Newman</td><td>2</td><td>1944–1955</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="rank-num">8</td><td class="composer-name">Miklós Rózsa</td><td>2</td><td>1946–1959</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="rank-num">8</td><td class="composer-name">Franz Waxman</td><td>2</td><td>1950–1951</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="rank-num">8</td><td class="composer-name">Nino Rota</td><td>2</td><td>1972–1974</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="rank-num">8</td><td class="composer-name">Hans Zimmer</td><td>2</td><td>1994–2021</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="rank-num">8</td><td class="composer-name">Howard Shore</td><td>2</td><td>2001–2003</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="rank-num">8</td><td class="composer-name">Gustavo Santaolalla</td><td>2</td><td>2005–2006</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="rank-num">8</td><td class="composer-name">Trent Reznor</td><td>2</td><td>2010–2020</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="rank-num">8</td><td class="composer-name">Atticus Ross</td><td>2</td><td>2010–2020</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="rank-num">8</td><td class="composer-name">Alexandre Desplat</td><td>2</td><td>2014–2017</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div class="chart-wrap">
      <h3>Wins by Top Composers</h3>
      <canvas id="chartTopComposers"></canvas>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>The three-way tie at 4 wins each is a relatively recent development. As recently as 2020, the record stood alone with John Williams at 4 (his last coming in 1993 for <em>Schindler's List</em>). John Barry reached 4 in 1990 with <em>Dances with Wolves</em>, and Göransson surged to 4 in just eight years — the most compressed accumulation of wins by any composer in Oscar history.</p>

  <p>Göransson's rapid ascent is historically unprecedented. His four wins (<em>Black Panther</em> 2018, <em>Oppenheimer</em> 2023 and 2024, <em>Sinners</em> 2026) came within a single decade, a pace unmatched even during the peak dominance periods of Max Steiner or Alan Menken. Where Menken needed 4 years to reach 3 wins and then stopped, Göransson's trajectory remains active.</p>

  <div class="highlight-box">
    <p><strong>Key insight:</strong> Repeat winners are not evenly distributed. Only 17 of 79 composers (21.5%) have won more than once, yet those 17 have claimed 31 of 93 awards. The category is shaped by a small elite whose dominance concentrates in specific eras.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- Section 3: Temporal Patterns -->
<section class="section" id="temporal-patterns">
  <h2>Temporal Patterns &amp; Decade-by-Decade Breakdown</h2>
  <p class="section-meta">How wins distribute across nine decades, and what that reveals about competition and concentration</p>

  <p class="lead">The decade-level view reveals a clear oscillation between periods of composer concentration and periods of wide-open competition. The <em>1940s</em> and <em>1980s</em> tied for the most diverse winner pools (12 unique composers each), while the <em>1950s</em> were the most concentrated (only 7 unique winners across 10 ceremonies).</p>

  <div class="chart-wrap">
    <h3>Unique Composers per Decade vs. Ceremonies per Decade</h3>
    <canvas id="chartDecades"></canvas>
  </div>

  <div class="two-col">
    <div class="chart-wrap">
      <h3>First-Time Winners per Decade</h3>
      <canvas id="chartFirstTimers"></canvas>
    </div>
    <div class="chart-wrap">
      <h3>Repeat Winner Rate by Decade</h3>
      <canvas id="chartRepeatRate"></canvas>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>The 1950s stand out as the era of maximum concentration. Only 7 distinct composers won across 10 ceremonies, with Dimitri Tiomkin claiming 3 and Alfred Newman, Miklós Rózsa, and Franz Waxman each taking 2. In contrast, the 1940s saw 12 unique winners — the highest count alongside the 1980s — reflecting both World War II's disruption of established careers and a postwar influx of émigré composers. The 1980s match this at 12 unique winners, driven by a wave of diverse entries including first-time Oscar winners from pop music (Vangelis, Herbie Hancock) and international cinema (Ryuichi Sakamoto, David Byrne, Cong Su for <em>The Last Emperor</em>).</p>

  <p>The repeat-winner rate peaked in the <em>1950s at 50%</em> — half of that decade's ceremonies were won by composers who had already held an Oscar. The rate dropped in subsequent decades: 30% in the 1960s, 30% in the 1970s, and 20% in both the 1980s and 1990s, before climbing again to 30% in the 2000s and 2010s. The 2020s (only 7 ceremonies so far) show a 42.9% repeat rate, but the small sample size makes this difficult to interpret.</p>

  <p class="note">Note: The 2020s include only 7 ceremonies (2020–2026). Per-ceremony metrics are comparable, but per-decade counts should be interpreted with the incomplete decade in mind.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 4: Era Analysis -->
<section class="section" id="era-analysis">
  <h2>Era-Based Analysis of Winning Scores</h2>
  <p class="section-meta">Characterizing each era's defining composers, films, and stylistic shifts</p>

  <p class="lead">Using approximate boundaries derived from widely accepted film-music historiography, we divide the 93 ceremonies into four eras: <em>Golden Age</em> (1934–1960), <em>Silver Age</em> (1961–1980), <em>Modern</em> (1981–2000), and <em>Contemporary</em> (2001–2026). Each era tells a distinct story about who won, and what kind of film carried the award.</p>

  <div class="era-grid">
    <div class="era-card golden">
      <h4 style="color:var(--era-golden)">Golden Age</h4>
      <p style="color:var(--text-dim);font-size:0.85em;margin-bottom:12px">1934 – 1960 &nbsp;·&nbsp; 27 Ceremonies</p>
