  <div class="chart-wrap">
    <h3>Era Comparison: Composers, Ceremonies, and Concentration</h3>
    <canvas id="chartEras"></canvas>
  </div>

  <p><strong>Golden Age (1934–1960): The Studio System Orchestra.</strong> This era was defined by the classical Hollywood studio system, where composers were staff employees or regular contractors. Max Steiner and Dimitri Tiomkin each won 3 times. The era had the highest number of ceremonies (27) and the most unique winners (26), reflecting the sheer volume of orchestral scores produced during Hollywood's classical period. Notable: Erich Wolfgang Korngold's 1938 win for <em>The Adventures of Robin Hood</em> cemented the swashbuckling symphonic style that defined prestige film scoring for decades.</p>

  <p><strong>Silver Age (1961–1980): The Melodist &amp; the Modernist.</strong> This era saw the arrival of distinct melodic voices — Henry Mancini (<em>Breakfast at Tiffany's</em>), John Barry (<em>Born Free</em>), and Maurice Jarre (<em>Lawrence of Arabia</em>, <em>Doctor Zhivago</em>). John Williams entered with <em>Jaws</em> (1975) and <em>Star Wars</em> (1977), signaling a return to large orchestral scores after a period where smaller, song-driven scores (Burt Bacharach's <em>Butch Cassidy</em>, Francis Lai's <em>Love Story</em>) had prevailed. The repeat-winner rate dropped to 20%, reflecting a broadening of the Academy's taste.</p>

  <p><strong>Modern (1981–2000): The Williams-Menken Era.</strong> John Williams added two more Oscars (<em>E.T.</em>, <em>Schindler's List</em>), but the defining story of this era was Alan Menken's three wins in four years for Disney animated features — <em>The Little Mermaid</em> (1989), <em>Beauty and the Beast</em> (1991), and <em>Aladdin</em> (1992). This concentration was anomalous: a single composer winning 3 of 4 consecutive years was unprecedented and has not been repeated. The era also featured Hans Zimmer's first Oscar (<em>The Lion King</em>, 1994).</p>

  <p><strong>Contemporary (2001–2026): Global Voices &amp; Synth Auteurs.</strong> The Contemporary era is the most stylistically diverse. Howard Shore's two wins for <em>The Lord of the Rings</em> trilogy validated fantasy scoring at the highest level. Gustavo Santaolalla's back-to-back wins (<em>Brokeback Mountain</em>, <em>Babel</em>) brought minimalist guitar-driven scores to the podium. Trent Reznor and Atticus Ross (<em>The Social Network</em>, <em>Soul</em>) bridged industrial rock and film scoring. And Ludwig Göransson's four wins demonstrated that genre versatility — from superhero (<em>Black Panther</em>) to historical drama (<em>Oppenheimer</em>) to period epic (<em>Sinners</em>) — is the defining trait of the era's most dominant composer. International voices also expanded: A.R. Rahman (<em>Slumdog Millionaire</em>), Hildur Guðnadóttir (<em>Joker</em>), and Volker Bertelmann (<em>All Quiet on the Western Front</em>) all claimed first-time wins.</p>

  <p class="note">Era boundaries follow film-music historiography conventions: Golden Age (classical studio system), Silver Age (New Hollywood &amp; international expansion), Modern (blockbuster era &amp; Disney renaissance), Contemporary (digital/post-genre).</p>
