<!-- Section 6: Conclusions -->
<section class="section" id="conclusions">
  <h2>Key Takeaways &amp; Forward-Looking Observations</h2>
  <p class="section-meta">What 93 years of Oscar-winning scores tell us about the past — and the future</p>

  <div class="takeaways">
    <div class="takeaway-card">
      <h4>1. Concentration is accelerating within individuals, not just the category</h4>
      <p>The all-time wins record stood at 4 from 1990 through 2026 — but <em>how</em> composers reach 4 has changed dramatically. Göransson's 8-year sprint to 4 wins is unprecedented and suggests that a composer who achieves both critical and commercial resonance today can accumulate Oscars faster than at any point in the category's history. The Contemporary era has produced more repeat winners (7 individuals with 2+ wins across 26 ceremonies) than the Modern era (4 individuals across 20 ceremonies), suggesting the category may be becoming <em>more</em> concentrated on a small elite, not less.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="takeaway-card">
      <h4>2. The "Disney effect" is era-specific, not permanent</h4>
      <p>Alan Menken's three wins for Disney animated features (1989–1992) felt at the time like a structural shift — the Academy permanently favoring the Disney musical. But no Disney animated feature has won Best Original Score since <em>Aladdin</em> in 1992. The pattern was not a new normal but a specific moment when the Disney Renaissance intersected with a relatively thin field of orchestral contenders. Since then, the Academy has favored dramatic scores, fantasy epics, and — increasingly — genre-blending work by composers who cross between film, pop, and electronic music.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="takeaway-card">
      <h4>3. The winner pool is globalizing but not yet diversifying in gender</h4>
      <p>Since 2000, winning composers have come from Iceland (Guðnadóttir), India (Rahman), China (Tan Dun), France (Desplat, Bource), Italy (Piovani, Morricone), Germany (Bertelmann), Canada (Shore, Danna), Sweden (Göransson), and Argentina (Santaolalla). The category is genuinely international. However, Hildur Guðnadóttir (2019) remains the <em>only</em> solo woman to win in 93 ceremonies. Four women appear in shared wins (as part of multi-composer teams for <em>Stagecoach</em>, <em>Pinocchio</em>, <em>Dumbo</em>, and <em>Mary Poppins</em> in the 1930s–1960s), but no woman won as a solo recipient until 2019.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="takeaway-card">
      <h4>4. The 2020s are on track to be the most concentrated decade since the 1950s</h4>
      <p>Through 7 of 10 ceremonies (2020–2026), the decade has already produced 7 unique winners. Ludwig Göransson alone accounts for 3 of those 7 ceremonies. If this pace holds, the 2020s could end with one of the lowest unique-winner counts of any completed decade. The key variable is whether Göransson's dominance continues across the remaining ceremonies (2027–2029) and whether any other composer can build a multi-win streak.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="takeaway-card">
      <h4>5. The category's identity has shifted from "best orchestral score" to "most impactful musical contribution to a film"</h4>
      <p>The early decades rewarded the prestige orchestral score almost exclusively (Steiner, Korngold, Tiomkin, Rózsa). By the 1960s and 1970s, the Academy had embraced jazz-inflected scores (Mancini, Bacharach), pop songwriters (Legrand, Hamlisch), and synthesizer-driven work (Moroder, Vangelis). The Contemporary era has added ambient minimalism (Santaolalla), industrial electronica (Reznor/Ross), and hip-hop-inflected orchestral work (Göransson's <em>Black Panther</em>). The through-line is no longer a specific musical language but an increasing openness to whatever musical approach best serves the film's narrative world — a definitional expansion that shows no signs of reversing.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="highlight-box" style="margin-top:32px">
    <p><strong>Looking ahead:</strong> Ludwig Göransson, at 41 years old with 4 Oscars already, is poised to break the all-time record — potentially within the next 5–7 years if his current pace continues. The question is not whether someone will reach 5 wins, but how soon, and whether the Academy's broadening taste will produce a genuinely diverse next generation of repeat winners or further consolidate around a small number of trusted names.</p>
  </div>
</section>

